# Code Rush — Web Dev Track (project memory)

**Theme:** Real-time crisis ops, maps, and AI. **Time limit:** 8 hours (competition context).

This repo also follows **CodeRush 2026 — Web Development** hackathon rules below (event policy + problem spec together).

## CodeRush 2026 — Event & governance

- **Format:** Full-stack web development; **8-hour continuous sprint**; **on-campus** hackathon; **up to 3** team members.
- **Problem:** Specific problem statement is issued at event start; build a **functional, responsive, dynamic** web app that solves it.
- **Changing requirements:** Module mimics real-world engineering — **requirements may change** during the sprint; design for **adaptability**.
- **Prizes (context only):** 1st PKR 30,000; 2nd PKR 15,000.

### Technical stack & tools

- **Allowed:** Open stack — e.g. MERN, MEAN, Django, Laravel, Next.js, etc.
- **Banned:** No-code / low-code (WordPress, Wix, Webflow, Bubble.io, etc.).
- **Start from scratch:** No pre-built templates pulled from drives; **standard CLI initializers** (e.g. `create-react-app`, `npm init`) are **allowed**.

### AI & internet policy

- **Generative AI:** Allowed (ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, etc.).
- **Internet:** Allowed (docs, Stack Overflow, forums).
- **Human assistance:** **Strictly prohibited** (outside team rules as enforced by organizers).
- **Communication tools:** **Banned** during sprint (WhatsApp, Discord, Telegram, Slack, etc. — per official rules).

### Mid-sprint twist

- A **surprise feature** lands at the **4-hour mark**; it **must** ship in the final solution.
- **Clean, modular code** makes integration easier.

### Submission & deployment

- **Platform:** **GitHub Classroom** (private team repo).
- **“Works on localhost” is NOT enough** — a **live, working URL is mandatory** for this hackathon.
- Deploy via **Vercel, Netlify, Heroku, GitHub Pages, AWS**, or similar acceptable hosts.
- **Commits:** Regular **incremental** commits expected; **one huge dump commit** may be flagged.

### Hackathon evaluation (high level)

- **Functionality & completeness** — solves the main problem.
- **Adaptability** — twist feature integrated successfully.
- **Deployment** — live link works.
- **UI/UX** — polished and **responsive**.

## Scenario

Major shipping chokepoint (Strait of Hormuz area) is a high-risk red zone. **Exactly 15** commercial cargo ships in transit; build the **command system**: live tracking, **role-based** control (fleet command vs ship captains), **AI** for distress, **weather-aware** rerouting, and **alerts** when ships drift into trouble.

## Non-negotiable numbers (grading)

- **15** active ships (no more, no less).
- Simulator / updates: **≥ 1 Hz** (tick at 1 Hz or faster).
- Ship state must reach all watchers **within 500 ms, 95%** of the time.
- **Geofence breach** alerts **≤ 1 s** after boundary cross.
- **Proximity:** warn when any two ships are **within 2 km** (continuous pair checks).
- **Adverse weather:** **+30% fuel** burn while moving through it.
- **≥ 5** concurrent viewers same fleet without state going out of sync.
- Client: **interpolate** movement between server updates — **smooth motion**, **no teleporting**, respect **max speed** / physically plausible motion.

## Data source (`fleet.json`)

Single shared dataset for all teams:

- `boundingBox`, `navigableWater` polygon (paths must stay in navigable water).
- `ports` with `id`, `name`, `position` `[lat, lng]`.
- `fleet`: 15 ships — each has `shipId`, `name`, `position`, `speed` (knots), `heading` (deg true), `destination` (port id), `fuel` (tons), `cargo`, `status`.

**Coordinate format:** `[lat, lng]`. **Hard-coded data** only where spec allows (e.g. basemap tiles). Ship state, alerts, AI output must be **live**.

## Backend simulator (you implement)

- No real fleet: **backend-owned state** advanced each tick.
- Each ship: position, speed, heading, destination port, fuel, cargo, operational status (e.g. normal, rerouting, distressed, stopped, stranded, insufficient fuel — as needed by spec).

### Routing

- **You** compute paths (not baked in config). Paths must remain in **navigable water** and avoid **restricted zones** operators draw at runtime.
- Algorithm is yours (A*, grid, visibility graph, deflection, etc.); **behavior** is graded.

**Auto-reroute (no operator approval):** new restricted zone **intersects** ship’s **current path** → status → rerouting → new path avoiding all zones.

**Captain / directive reroute:** command issues directive → captain **ACCEPT** or **ESCALATE_DISTRESS** → on ACCEPT, **next tick** routing picks up new goal (different port, waypoint, hold).

**Weather:** not a standalone trigger; bad weather = fuel penalty. Avoiding a storm = normal **directive / reroute** path.

### Edge cases

- **No valid path** (e.g. zones box in destination) → **stranded** + alert.
- **Zone drawn around a ship already inside** → **geofence breach** + attempt reroute out.
- **Insufficient fuel** for destination on new path → flag status, **still move** until fuel actually runs out.

### Each tick (≥ 1 Hz)

- Advance each ship along current heading: distance = **speed × tick duration** (consistent units).
- Apply **+30% fuel** when in **adverse weather**.
- Update statuses (out of fuel, in zone, arrived, etc.).
- **Push** new state to all connected clients (see real-time).

## Map & UI

- Interactive ocean map: **all 15 ships**.
- **Command:** draw/edit **polygonal restricted zones** at runtime.
- **Captain:** sees zones, **cannot** add/edit.
- **Restricted zone entry:** alert (**visual + audible**) until **acknowledged or resolved**.
- Ship click: **cargo, fuel, speed, destination, operational status**.

## Real-time

- **WebSocket** (or equivalent persistent connection) — **no polling**.
- **Single source of truth**; everyone sees the same fleet state.
- Client **interpolation** between updates (smooth, capped speed, no jumps).

## Roles & flows

- **Command:** full fleet; directives to any ship; **only** role that manages restricted zones.
- **Captain:** **one** ship; receives directives; responds **ACCEPT** (course updates next tick) or **ESCALATE_DISTRESS** (free-form distress message). Directive + response visible to **everyone** **immediately**.

## AI / NLP

- Free-form distress messages from captains.
- Extract **structured** fields: severity, problem, quantifiable impact (injuries, damage estimates, etc.).
- Use extraction to **prioritize / display** alerts system-wide.

## Weather & routing

- Pull **real** weather (e.g. Open-Meteo, Stormglass, or free-tier API).
- **+30% fuel** when moving through adverse weather.
- When recomputing path, **factor in current weather** (avoid worst where routing allows).
- Report whether ship **can reach destination** with current fuel given path + conditions.

## Proximity

- Continuous pairwise checks; **2 km** threshold; same **alert pipeline** as other alerts.

## Playback / timeline

- Persist history (event log, snapshots, ring buffer, etc.).
- **Timeline UI:** scrub back; **last 1 hour** at **≥ 30 s** resolution is enough.
- Full reconstruction at arbitrary timestamps **not** required.

## Running & deliverables

- **`docker compose up`** (from repo **root**) brings entire system up (problem spec).
- **README:** document API keys / env vars.
- Submit: **GitHub Classroom repo link**, **`docker-compose.yml` in root** (problem spec), and a **deployed live URL** — **mandatory for CodeRush 2026** (not localhost-only).

## Bonuses (+5% each, additive, cap 110%)

Only after core is stable: multiple reroute candidates; ship-to-ship assistance; predictive “before it breaks” alerts; proactive AI fleet advisor with accept/reject.

## Grading weights

Core E2E + consistent real-time state **60%**; AI/NLP depth **20%**; geo + UI/UX **15%**; code quality **5%**.

## Ground rules

- Must run **end-to-end on a laptop**; no undisclosed cloud-only dependencies.
- If spec silent: **document assumptions** in README; undocumented assumptions judged strictly.

---

When implementing this repo, treat **both** the problem spec and **CodeRush 2026** sections as **source of truth**; ship a **working deploy**, **incremental git history**, **modular** layout for the 4-hour twist, and stack choices compatible with **Docker**, **WebSockets**, **live** ship/alert/AI data, and **fleet.json** geometry.
